WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.740
Welcome to the Deep Dive. Today we are setting out on, well, a very specific mission, honestly.

00:06.420 --> 00:08.860
Yeah, a pretty massive one when you get down to it.

00:08.940 --> 00:15.400
Exactly. We are going to unpack how we, meaning you, me, readers, editors, or just, you know,

00:15.480 --> 00:20.940
everyday consumers of information, how we actually judge the intellectual merit of an idea

00:20.940 --> 00:25.200
when the very definition of authorship is being completely stretched.

00:25.680 --> 00:28.300
Right. Stretched almost beyond recognition at this point.

00:28.300 --> 00:36.980
Because we are entering this era where machine-assisted workflows are just completely shattering our traditional understanding of what it means to, you know, write something.

00:37.500 --> 00:37.580
Yeah.

00:37.800 --> 00:45.940
And if the physical act of typing is no longer the bottleneck of thought, then, well, how do we evaluate the thinking itself?

00:46.640 --> 00:48.880
That's the huge question right now.

00:48.960 --> 00:49.220
Mm-hmm.

00:49.720 --> 00:57.100
And to navigate this, we are looking at two highly influential pieces of writing that are really shaping this exact debate as we speak.

00:57.100 --> 00:59.820
Yeah, the sources for today are just fascinating.

01:00.040 --> 01:00.400
They are.

01:01.400 --> 01:07.600
So our grounding text is an October 2026 paper by an independent researcher who publishes under the name Flyxion.

01:07.860 --> 01:13.040
It's titled, The Unit of Scrutiny, Delegated Inscription and Workflow Authorship.

01:13.360 --> 01:15.160
A bit of a mouthful, but we'll break it down.

01:15.480 --> 01:16.080
Oh, absolutely.

01:16.080 --> 01:25.820
But what makes Flyxion's work so vital is that it's actually a direct, like, a really profound response to a recent article by Jean Desperment.

01:26.340 --> 01:26.740
Right.

01:26.900 --> 01:29.040
The one published on the Volker X blog.

01:29.400 --> 01:29.780
Exactly.

01:30.340 --> 01:33.860
So our goal today is to kind of bridge both of their arguments.

01:34.440 --> 01:41.280
Because, you know, Desperment establishes this fundamental rule for how we should view artificial intelligence when writing a single essay.

01:41.580 --> 01:42.580
The traditional format.

01:42.580 --> 01:43.060
Right.

01:43.840 --> 01:55.460
But then Flyxion steps in and basically asks, well, how do we navigate a world where scholars aren't writing essays anymore, but rather writing code that generates thousands of essays simultaneously?

01:55.740 --> 01:57.220
Which is just a wild concept.

01:57.760 --> 02:03.460
And to give you, the listener, a sense of the scale we are actually talking about here, I want you to imagine a scenario.

02:03.940 --> 02:04.700
Okay, lay it out.

02:04.700 --> 02:07.940
So, just picture a totally ordinary Tuesday afternoon.

02:08.420 --> 02:11.960
You are sitting at your desk, maybe drinking a cup of coffee.

02:13.000 --> 02:14.200
You open your laptop.

02:14.660 --> 02:18.100
You pull up a piece of code you've been, you know, tinkering with for a few weeks.

02:18.500 --> 02:20.260
Right, just normal coding stuff.

02:20.520 --> 02:20.660
Yeah.

02:21.240 --> 02:21.980
And you hit run.

02:22.600 --> 02:25.820
And then you walk into the kitchen to grab a snack or whatever.

02:25.820 --> 02:33.740
By the time you come back to your desk, you have just, quote unquote, produced 19 million essays.

02:34.400 --> 02:35.320
19 million.

02:35.920 --> 02:37.280
Just let that sink in.

02:37.520 --> 02:37.720
Right.

02:38.140 --> 02:41.700
And not 19 million bullet points or, like, vague concepts.

02:42.300 --> 02:49.340
19 million full, structured, meticulously cited outlines and prompts for academic papers.

02:49.600 --> 02:52.920
All generated from a lifetime of scholarly work.

02:53.100 --> 02:53.740
It's unbelievable.

02:53.740 --> 03:01.520
So, the question is, as the person sitting in that chair, staring at that terminal window, are you the author of those 19 million essays?

03:02.120 --> 03:03.780
Like, where did the actual thinking happen?

03:04.140 --> 03:06.640
It is such a staggering thought experiment.

03:07.120 --> 03:09.900
But the crazy thing is, it isn't science fiction anymore.

03:10.020 --> 03:12.540
It's the exact reality that Flyxion is addressing.

03:13.000 --> 03:13.220
Yeah.

03:13.740 --> 03:22.420
But to truly understand the sheer, you know, paradigm shift of generating 19 million outlines, we actually have to start with the baseline argument first.

03:22.420 --> 03:24.220
Right. We have to walk before we can run.

03:24.560 --> 03:35.140
Exactly. We have to look at the conflict Flyxion is responding to in the first place, which requires us to really understand Dassault's foundational view on humans, machines, and the single text.

03:35.660 --> 03:37.480
Okay. So, let's lay that foundation.

03:37.480 --> 03:45.380
Because Dasspermann is looking at the current climate in academia and publishing, which he characterizes, honestly, fairly bluntly, as a witch hunt.

03:45.880 --> 03:46.880
A total witch hunt. Yeah.

03:47.400 --> 03:53.520
There is this pervasive, like, almost paranoid obsession right now with trying to catch people using language models.

03:53.900 --> 03:55.480
Right. The whole detection panic.

03:55.480 --> 04:06.700
Exactly. Every editor, every peer reviewer, every teacher is running texts through these so-called AI detectors, just desperate to figure out if a machine contributed to the prose.

04:07.460 --> 04:14.940
And Dasspermann's core thesis is that this desperation is fundamentally misguided. Like, we are aggressively asking the wrong question here.

04:15.080 --> 04:15.440
How so?

04:15.440 --> 04:24.860
Well, when you look at a manuscript and you ask, did a machine write this, you are essentially treating the mechanical generation of words as the defining feature of scholarship.

04:25.140 --> 04:25.940
Which it isn't.

04:26.040 --> 04:33.600
Exactly. Dasspermann argues the only question that has ever mattered, and the only question that matters right now, is, does this text think?

04:34.020 --> 04:36.880
I just love that phrasing so much. Does the text think?

04:36.880 --> 04:45.160
Because it forces a distinction between two very different actions, right? Delegating inscription versus delegating thought.

04:45.520 --> 04:52.020
Yes. And this distinction is what Flyxion refers to as Dasspermann's compass. It's how we navigate this.

04:52.500 --> 05:01.300
So inscription is just the rendering. It is the mechanical, physical, or, you know, digital act of putting words onto a page to represent an idea.

05:01.800 --> 05:02.660
The typing part.

05:02.660 --> 05:16.360
Exactly. Thought, on the other hand, is the formation of the idea itself. It's the synthesis of information, the novel connection between two completely disparate concepts, the actual architecture of the argument.

05:16.600 --> 05:18.200
The heavy lifting. Mentally.

05:18.540 --> 05:26.420
Right. And Dasspermann asserts that delegating the inscription, the mere typing or phrasing of a concept, is perfectly unobjectionable.

05:26.680 --> 05:27.740
Right. Who cares who typed it?

05:27.740 --> 05:32.620
Exactly. The danger only arises when you delegate the thought-forming process.

05:33.080 --> 05:37.720
You know, hearing you describe that separation, it immediately makes me think of, like, a commercial kitchen.

05:38.120 --> 05:39.140
Oh, that's a good comparison.

05:39.360 --> 05:47.700
Right. Think about a high-end Michelin-starred restaurant. You have an executive chef, and that chef spends months conceptualizing a tasting menu.

05:47.960 --> 05:50.000
Developing the menu, testing flavors, yeah.

05:50.280 --> 05:53.180
Exactly. They figure out the exact flavor profile they want.

05:53.180 --> 06:00.280
Like, maybe they realize the acidity of a fermented plum will perfectly balance the richness of a specific cut of duck.

06:00.980 --> 06:05.040
And they envision this plating design that looks like a forest floor, or whatever.

06:05.480 --> 06:06.040
Very fancy.

06:06.600 --> 06:07.160
Super fancy.

06:07.580 --> 06:11.200
But that entire conceptualization, that is the thought, right?

06:11.380 --> 06:12.660
Yes, the intellectual labor.

06:12.660 --> 06:20.500
But then, when the Friday night dinner rush actually hits, that executive chef is not standing in the back mechanically peeling 300 potatoes.

06:20.800 --> 06:21.800
No, definitely not.

06:22.080 --> 06:24.280
They aren't chopping massive piles of onions.

06:24.520 --> 06:29.600
They delegate that to a sous chef, or a prep cook, or even, you know, a mechanical food processor.

06:30.260 --> 06:31.760
They hand off the mechanical rendering.

06:32.280 --> 06:32.600
Right.

06:32.600 --> 06:40.340
And when that plate hits the table, nobody sends it back demanding to know if the executive chef personally held the paring knife to slice the plum.

06:40.860 --> 06:44.960
The meal is valid because the executive chef retained the thought-forming process.

06:45.360 --> 06:51.920
That is a highly illustrative way to look at it, because the absurdity of judging the dish by the knife used for the prep work,

06:52.140 --> 06:57.580
it completely mirrors the absurdity of judging a scholar by the tool used to smooth out their prose.

06:57.740 --> 06:58.140
Exactly.

06:58.140 --> 07:05.660
And de Ackermann contextualizes this beautifully by placing our current AI panic into a very long historical continuum,

07:05.660 --> 07:11.200
because we always feel that our specific technological crisis is entirely unprecedented, right?

07:11.320 --> 07:11.820
Oh, always.

07:12.080 --> 07:13.620
It's the end of the world every time.

07:14.260 --> 07:17.540
But the anxiety over delegating inscription is actually ancient.

07:18.440 --> 07:21.480
Yeah, the paper specifically brings up Plato, which I found so fascinating.

07:21.860 --> 07:24.220
We are talking about the Phaedrus dialogue, right?

07:24.460 --> 07:24.840
Yes.

07:24.840 --> 07:29.440
Where Socrates tells the story of the Egyptian god Thoth inventing written letters.

07:29.860 --> 07:30.260
Exactly.

07:30.820 --> 07:35.200
So the invention of writing itself was viewed as a catastrophic delegation back then.

07:35.280 --> 07:35.500
Writing.

07:36.240 --> 07:37.440
Just basic writing.

07:37.820 --> 07:38.180
I know.

07:39.120 --> 07:43.840
Thoth presents writing to the king, Thomas, claiming it will improve memory and wisdom.

07:44.340 --> 07:47.000
But Thomas vehemently disagrees.

07:47.280 --> 07:48.280
He hates the idea.

07:48.440 --> 07:49.220
He does.

07:49.220 --> 07:54.020
He argues that if humans externalize their knowledge onto a scroll, like if they delegate

07:54.020 --> 07:58.980
the holding of information to a piece of papyrus, they will basically cease to exercise their memory.

07:59.620 --> 07:59.640
Right.

07:59.700 --> 08:01.320
They won't need to remember anything anymore.

08:01.520 --> 08:01.840
Exactly.

08:02.600 --> 08:08.020
He says they will become forgetful, relying on external marks rather than internal understanding.

08:08.440 --> 08:11.780
They will have the appearance of wisdom without the reality of it.

08:12.000 --> 08:14.160
It's literally the exact same argument we're here today.

08:14.940 --> 08:18.580
Like, if the machine writes the essay, the student won't know how to think.

08:18.580 --> 08:20.420
The parallels are just striking.

08:20.900 --> 08:20.980
Yeah.

08:21.220 --> 08:25.620
And this cycle, it repeats with every single major leap in inscription technology.

08:26.200 --> 08:27.120
Give me another example.

08:27.940 --> 08:32.420
Consider the transition from manuscript culture to the printing press in the 15th century.

08:32.960 --> 08:33.440
Oh, right.

08:33.560 --> 08:33.980
The monks.

08:34.380 --> 08:34.660
Right.

08:35.360 --> 08:40.840
In scriptoriums across Europe, monks viewed the physical, laborious act of hand-copying text

08:40.840 --> 08:44.820
not just as reproduction, but as this deeply spiritual exercise.

08:45.200 --> 08:46.260
Like it was part of the devotion.

08:46.760 --> 08:47.260
Exactly.

08:47.260 --> 08:52.560
The painful slowness of copying a Bible by hand was seen as a way of internalizing the

08:52.560 --> 08:53.160
divine word.

08:53.840 --> 08:57.640
So when the printing press arrived, there was intense clerical alarm.

08:58.500 --> 08:59.200
Because it was too easy.

08:59.840 --> 09:00.180
Yes.

09:00.460 --> 09:05.900
The mechanical stamping of ink onto paper was viewed by many as soulless, like this corruption

09:05.900 --> 09:09.760
that severed the physical, spiritual link between the creator and the text.

09:10.720 --> 09:11.280
Wow.

09:11.280 --> 09:16.260
But over time, the printing press just became, you know, a printing press, a tool.

09:16.580 --> 09:16.920
Exactly.

09:17.180 --> 09:22.400
Which kind of brings us to the late 20th century, where we absorbed an entirely new set of invisible

09:22.400 --> 09:24.220
technologies without even blinking.

09:24.900 --> 09:25.200
Right.

09:25.440 --> 09:28.360
Word processors, spell checkers, grammar software.

09:28.360 --> 09:28.860
Yeah.

09:28.860 --> 09:35.980
If we go back just like 30 or 40 years, the idea of using a machine to instantly organize a bibliography

09:35.980 --> 09:40.340
like we do with Zotero or EndNote today, that was considered lazy.

09:40.740 --> 09:42.000
You were supposed to do it by hand.

09:42.140 --> 09:42.420
Right.

09:42.640 --> 09:47.540
You were supposed to memorize the Chicago Manual of Style and type out your index cards manually.

09:48.200 --> 09:52.820
Today, if you don't use a citation manager, you aren't viewed as intellectually superior.

09:52.820 --> 09:55.220
You're just viewed as incredibly inefficient.

09:56.300 --> 09:56.580
Exactly.

09:57.260 --> 10:04.080
And this historical amnesia, it points to a deeply ingrained myth that Desperon deconstructs

10:04.080 --> 10:09.720
and which Flyxion thoroughly endorses, which is the myth of the sovereign author.

10:10.180 --> 10:11.120
The sovereign author.

10:11.500 --> 10:11.760
Yes.

10:12.220 --> 10:17.540
We harbor this romanticized, almost cinematic image of the lone genius.

10:17.540 --> 10:22.520
You know, the brilliant philosopher isolated in a cabin in the woods, pulling fully formed,

10:22.680 --> 10:27.300
unadulterated ideas from the ether and just pouring them directly onto the page without

10:27.300 --> 10:28.680
any mediation at all.

10:28.860 --> 10:30.400
It's total Hemingway trope stuff.

10:30.760 --> 10:35.900
Just a brilliant mind, a typewriter, a bottle of whiskey, and sheer willpower giving birth

10:35.900 --> 10:37.940
to a masterpiece in total isolation.

10:38.440 --> 10:41.700
But that figure is entirely retroactive fiction.

10:42.220 --> 10:45.580
True authorship, especially in academia, is never solitary.

10:45.580 --> 10:48.820
It is this dense, overlapping network.

10:49.040 --> 10:49.640
It takes a village.

10:49.940 --> 10:55.640
It involves scribes, editors, peer reviewers, random, casual conversations in the hallway,

10:55.900 --> 10:58.100
and most importantly, prior texts.

10:58.200 --> 10:58.340
Right.

10:58.400 --> 10:59.280
Nobody writes in a vacuum.

10:59.420 --> 10:59.900
Exactly.

11:00.440 --> 11:04.120
Every piece of writing is a reaction to something written before it.

11:04.480 --> 11:09.900
The idea of a text having a single, uncontaminated point of origin is just a fantasy.

11:10.360 --> 11:13.840
We are always reliant on the intellectual infrastructure built by others,

11:13.840 --> 11:17.220
and we continually adopt new tools to navigate that infrastructure.

11:17.300 --> 11:17.820
Okay.

11:18.220 --> 11:23.280
So if we accept that the lone genius is a total myth, and that delegating the, you know,

11:23.440 --> 11:28.820
chopping of the onions is a historically normal progression, why is the current reaction to

11:28.820 --> 11:30.260
AI so uniquely harmful?

11:30.780 --> 11:34.740
What makes this a witch hunt rather than just like a normal adjustment period for a new tool?

11:34.740 --> 11:38.980
The harm really lies in how we are attempting to enforce this separation.

11:39.740 --> 11:45.220
Because editors and readers cannot physically peer over a scholar's shoulder to see their thought process,

11:45.680 --> 11:49.680
they have developed a deeply flawed detection culture.

11:50.220 --> 11:51.200
The AI detectors.

11:51.540 --> 11:51.760
Right.

11:52.760 --> 11:58.020
They are relying on stylistic proxies as prima facie evidence of intellectual contamination.

11:58.020 --> 12:01.840
And this is where things get genuinely ridiculous, in my opinion.

12:02.100 --> 12:06.260
We are talking about using punctuation as a lie detector test.

12:06.300 --> 12:07.080
It's absurd.

12:07.640 --> 12:10.100
The paper specifically highlights the M-dash.

12:10.300 --> 12:12.020
For anyone who isn't a massive grammar nerd,

12:12.400 --> 12:16.580
the M-dash is that long, horizontal line used to set off a thought or add emphasis,

12:17.080 --> 12:19.420
like a dramatic pause in text.

12:19.560 --> 12:19.780
Right.

12:19.780 --> 12:24.560
And suddenly, over the last few years, if an essay uses too many M-dashes,

12:24.960 --> 12:28.000
people are treating it as undeniable proof of AI generation.

12:28.480 --> 12:30.040
It isn't just the M-dash either.

12:30.240 --> 12:33.600
It's also the tricolon, the rhetorical rule of three.

12:34.160 --> 12:35.320
Like blood, sweat, and tears.

12:35.460 --> 12:35.800
Exactly.

12:35.960 --> 12:37.200
Or stop, drop, and roll.

12:37.680 --> 12:40.560
It also extends to certain transitional phrases like,

12:40.640 --> 12:44.040
it is crucial to note, or in summary.

12:44.780 --> 12:46.520
Phrases people have used forever.

12:46.720 --> 12:46.960
Right.

12:46.960 --> 12:50.880
But the logic behind this suspicion is that large language models

12:50.880 --> 12:53.340
were trained on vast swaths of human text.

12:54.020 --> 12:55.720
During their reinforcement learning phases,

12:55.980 --> 12:58.700
they learned what human evaluators consider helpful

12:58.700 --> 13:00.420
and well-structured academic writing.

13:01.000 --> 13:02.500
So they learned to write like us.

13:02.660 --> 13:03.020
Exactly.

13:03.820 --> 13:07.280
They absorbed our rhetorical habits, and they reproduced them very reliably.

13:07.820 --> 13:10.300
But the profound irony here, the thing that makes me crazy,

13:10.480 --> 13:13.540
is that those rhetorical devices are inherently human.

13:13.540 --> 13:18.680
Humans invented the rule of three centuries before computers even existed.

13:19.200 --> 13:20.360
Cicero used the tricolon.

13:20.700 --> 13:21.080
Exactly.

13:21.840 --> 13:25.620
If you happen to be a human scholar who genuinely enjoys using an M-dash

13:25.620 --> 13:27.380
to structure a complex sentence,

13:27.840 --> 13:32.420
your actual human effort is now suddenly under suspicion of being artificial.

13:33.160 --> 13:37.680
You are being flagged by a proxy that cannot possibly measure the origin of the thought.

13:37.680 --> 13:41.520
And the collateral damage of this proxy hunting is severe.

13:41.980 --> 13:44.560
It allows for the complete disqualification of a text

13:44.560 --> 13:48.060
without requiring any engagement with its actual argument.

13:48.440 --> 13:49.940
You just throw it out based on the punctuation?

13:50.100 --> 13:50.440
Yes.

13:51.380 --> 13:54.220
If an editor spots three M-dashes in the first paragraph

13:54.220 --> 13:56.560
and tosses the manuscript in the rejection pile,

13:56.680 --> 13:59.420
they never have to ask Daspermont's vital question.

13:59.840 --> 14:01.360
Does this text think?

14:01.360 --> 14:04.600
The stylistic proxy just becomes a really convenient excuse

14:04.600 --> 14:08.420
to avoid the hard, slow work of evaluating intellectual merit.

14:08.920 --> 14:13.120
So Daspermont's solution to this really messy, proxy-obsessed culture

14:13.120 --> 14:15.700
relies entirely on the individual scholar, right?

14:15.700 --> 14:16.720
Yes. Self-discipline.

14:16.840 --> 14:17.020
Right.

14:17.120 --> 14:20.660
If AI detectors don't work and stylistic proxies are unjust,

14:20.880 --> 14:23.660
the only safeguard left is self-discipline.

14:24.260 --> 14:26.760
The scholar must sit at their desk, draft their work,

14:26.760 --> 14:31.400
and maintain a rigorous sentence-by-sentence vigilance.

14:31.520 --> 14:32.560
Constantly checking themselves.

14:32.820 --> 14:34.280
Yeah. They have to constantly ask themselves,

14:34.680 --> 14:36.960
okay, did the machine just smooth out my sentence?

14:37.240 --> 14:40.440
Or did it just introduce a factual claim I didn't actually verify?

14:40.920 --> 14:42.600
Did it hallucinate a citation?

14:43.240 --> 14:45.160
Did it gloss over a gap in my logic?

14:45.700 --> 14:48.560
As long as the scholar polices that specific boundary,

14:49.020 --> 14:49.880
the text is valid.

14:50.100 --> 14:53.760
And look, for the traditional scholar writing a single journal article,

14:53.940 --> 14:55.200
that framework holds up perfectly.

14:55.620 --> 14:55.920
But?

14:55.920 --> 14:59.640
This is where we have to completely shatter the foundation.

14:59.660 --> 15:00.080
Here we go.

15:00.700 --> 15:06.280
Because Daspermont's entire defense rests on one colossal, unstated assumption.

15:07.200 --> 15:09.820
It assumes the unit of production is a single text.

15:10.120 --> 15:10.400
Right.

15:10.640 --> 15:14.160
It assumes I sit down, I write one essay, I submit it,

15:14.520 --> 15:16.160
and I stand behind it as its author.

15:16.960 --> 15:19.300
But what happens if the text isn't a text at all?

15:19.800 --> 15:22.040
What happens if the output is a program?

15:22.460 --> 15:25.060
And this is the exact pivot point of Flyxion's paper.

15:25.060 --> 15:28.560
Flyxion agrees with Daspermont completely regarding the single text.

15:28.560 --> 15:35.300
But we are rapidly moving into an era where the single text is no longer the default unit of scholarly production.

15:35.540 --> 15:38.720
We are entering the era of the generative workflow.

15:38.720 --> 15:44.040
Okay, if you're listening to this and wondering what a generative workflow actually looks like on the computer screen,

15:44.600 --> 15:45.560
let's break it down.

15:45.720 --> 15:49.300
Because this isn't just opening up ChatGPT and typing, write me an essay.

15:49.720 --> 15:50.580
No, not at all.

15:51.220 --> 15:52.600
That's a common misconception.

15:53.380 --> 15:57.060
In a generative workflow, the scholar is essentially acting as a programmer.

15:57.500 --> 16:00.560
They write a specification, which is a script, perhaps in Python.

16:00.560 --> 16:08.320
And the script acts as a conductor, orchestrating handoffs between several different AI models and databases automatically.

16:08.840 --> 16:10.280
So it's stringing tools together.

16:10.520 --> 16:10.860
Exactly.

16:11.540 --> 16:16.320
For example, the script might first ping an academic database to pull a hundred abstracts.

16:16.440 --> 16:22.560
It then feeds those abstracts into a language model with specific instructions to identify conflicting methodologies.

16:22.940 --> 16:23.460
Oh, okay.

16:23.460 --> 16:32.680
Then it extracts those conflicts, formats them into new prompts, hands those prompts back to the model to generate full-length essay outlines exploring the conflicts,

16:33.040 --> 16:37.840
and finally passes those outlines to another script that formats them into PDFs.

16:38.100 --> 16:41.080
All without the human clicking anything after hitting start.

16:41.480 --> 16:41.760
Right.

16:41.760 --> 16:53.540
So instead of one human action producing one sentence, a single action, which is executing the script, initiates a loop that can produce thousands of entirely distinct outputs.

16:54.060 --> 16:57.120
And Flyxion raises the critical epistemological problem here, right?

16:57.440 --> 17:06.140
When a single specification generates an enormous multitude of outputs, how on earth does Das Perrin's sentence-by-sentence self-discipline apply?

17:06.580 --> 17:07.180
It doesn't.

17:07.500 --> 17:07.700
Right.

17:07.700 --> 17:11.700
The primary question, does this generated text think?

17:12.280 --> 17:15.380
Suddenly becomes totally inadequate as our unit of scrutiny.

17:16.300 --> 17:20.660
But I have to stop you there because let me play devil's advocate for a second.

17:21.000 --> 17:25.580
I am really struggling to see how this isn't just a massive abdication of responsibility.

17:26.080 --> 17:27.280
That's a fair reaction.

17:27.880 --> 17:35.160
Because if I write a brilliant, complex, thousand-line Python script that dictates exactly how sources should be synthesized,

17:35.160 --> 17:41.240
and I hit run, and the AI goes off and generates the final prose for a thousand different essays,

17:41.640 --> 17:44.220
I haven't read 999 of them.

17:44.360 --> 17:45.460
No, you haven't.

17:45.620 --> 17:48.180
I physically haven't laid eyes on the arguments they are making.

17:48.680 --> 17:53.500
So how can I possibly claim authorship of something I haven't even read?

17:54.160 --> 17:56.900
Isn't that the literal definition of delegating thought?

17:56.900 --> 18:03.180
Your discomfort is entirely justified, and honestly, it points to exactly why Flyxion wrote the paper in the first place.

18:03.260 --> 18:03.500
Okay.

18:03.820 --> 18:12.400
Because if you generate a thousand texts and you do not read them, you absolutely cannot stand behind any single one of them as a finished, endorsed piece of scholarship.

18:12.940 --> 18:15.540
The text itself is unread by its creator.

18:16.020 --> 18:18.980
Therefore, the thinking did not happen in the text.

18:19.060 --> 18:19.720
And where did it happen?

18:19.720 --> 18:22.500
The locus of thought has shifted entirely.

18:23.080 --> 18:26.720
The intellectual contribution is no longer located in the prose of the output.

18:27.300 --> 18:34.000
It is located entirely in the design of the pipeline itself and in the subsequent selection among its outputs.

18:34.280 --> 18:34.680
Oh, I see.

18:35.160 --> 18:36.700
You didn't author a thousand essays.

18:37.120 --> 18:38.160
You authored a procedure.

18:38.840 --> 18:40.020
You authored a generator.

18:41.060 --> 18:47.180
Because the unit of production has expanded from the essay to the workflow, the unit of scrutiny must expand with it.

18:47.180 --> 18:52.240
We must evaluate the code, the parameters, and the constraints of the system you built.

18:52.600 --> 18:52.620
Okay.

18:52.740 --> 18:55.460
This is a heavy abstraction, so let's ground it.

18:56.020 --> 19:03.680
Because Flyxion provides three very distinct, highly detailed examples of these workflows to show us what this paradigm shift actually looks like in practice.

19:03.780 --> 19:04.980
They are great examples, too.

19:05.200 --> 19:05.380
Yeah.

19:05.580 --> 19:08.480
Let's look at the first one, which Flyxion calls the Screenplay Gallery.

19:08.700 --> 19:08.920
Right.

19:09.500 --> 19:14.420
So in the Screenplay Gallery workflow, the designer utilizes a moderately sized language model.

19:14.420 --> 19:18.120
The human's input is a catalog of highly specific premises.

19:18.520 --> 19:19.280
Premises for scripts?

19:19.560 --> 19:19.740
Yes.

19:19.900 --> 19:24.120
But importantly, these aren't generic prompts like write a romantic comedy.

19:24.860 --> 19:30.100
They are premises designed to illustrate complex mathematical or conceptual frameworks through narrative.

19:30.520 --> 19:38.620
For example, asking the model to write a scene that demonstrates the principles of non-Euclidean geometry through an argument between two architects.

19:38.800 --> 19:39.660
A perfect example.

19:39.660 --> 19:48.860
And for those who might need a quick refresher, non-Euclidean geometry involves spaces that are curved, unlike the flat planes of traditional geometry we learned in high school.

19:49.600 --> 19:58.160
In a non-Euclidean space, parallel lines might actually eventually intersect, or the angles of a triangle might not add up to 180 degrees.

19:58.460 --> 19:59.300
Which is hard to picture.

19:59.300 --> 20:00.300
Exactly.

20:00.640 --> 20:05.460
So trying to dramatize that mathematical reality requires a very specific type of logic.

20:06.240 --> 20:10.340
The language model is tasked with generating screenplays based on those conceptual premises.

20:11.060 --> 20:11.100
Okay.

20:11.660 --> 20:22.060
Then the workflow autonomously hands those generated screenplays over to an image generation model, which creates visual stills, like storyboards, for the scenes described in the text.

20:22.060 --> 20:25.540
So what is the human actually doing here then?

20:25.820 --> 20:31.200
Because they aren't writing the dialogue about intersecting parallel lines, and they certainly aren't drawing the storyboard pixels.

20:31.740 --> 20:31.860
Right.

20:32.220 --> 20:36.000
Flyxion points out that the designer's code is managing this sequence.

20:36.660 --> 20:49.840
It's managing the timing of the API calls, the variants requested at each stage to ensure diversity, the naming conventions of the files, and the final assembly of the text and images into a browsable gallery.

20:50.000 --> 20:50.600
It's structural.

20:50.600 --> 20:51.360
Exactly.

20:51.680 --> 20:57.800
The designer's intellectual contribution is the architecture of the stages and the overarching purpose of the assembly.

20:58.620 --> 21:01.060
The language model supplies the linguistic material.

21:01.440 --> 21:02.980
The image model interprets it visually.

21:03.240 --> 21:13.140
But the relations among these stages, the structural logic that makes it a cohesive gallery rather than random noise, that is dictated entirely by the human's code.

21:13.800 --> 21:16.540
You know, it reminds me of a television showrunner.

21:16.800 --> 21:17.540
Oh, that's interesting.

21:17.720 --> 21:17.900
Right?

21:17.900 --> 21:24.900
If you look at massive prestige TV series, the showrunner doesn't write every single line of dialogue in all 10 episodes.

21:25.360 --> 21:29.720
They certainly don't design every costume or frame every camera shot or adjust the lighting.

21:29.720 --> 21:31.780
No, they have huge teams for that.

21:31.920 --> 21:32.200
Exactly.

21:32.700 --> 21:42.620
But we still view the showrunner as the creative author of the series because their intellectual contribution is the vision, the structure, the delegation of tasks, and the final approval.

21:42.620 --> 21:49.700
In this workflow, the designer built the machine that created the show for a highly specific conceptual purpose.

21:49.700 --> 21:52.280
That analogy holds up incredibly well.

21:52.540 --> 21:57.160
A single person can now produce a catalog of several hundred multimedia projects.

21:57.760 --> 22:06.040
Not because they are working a million hours a week painting and typing, but because the workflow, once constructed, can execute the vision repeatedly.

22:06.600 --> 22:11.080
The direction is entirely traceable to the choices embodied in the script.

22:11.080 --> 22:12.820
Okay, that's the showrunner model.

22:13.320 --> 22:16.840
It stretches authorship, but I feel like we can wrap our heads around it.

22:17.260 --> 22:20.180
The second example, however, breaks the scale entirely.

22:20.340 --> 22:21.000
It really does.

22:21.500 --> 22:25.360
This is the 19 million essay machine I mentioned at the start of the deep dive.

22:25.840 --> 22:28.760
Walk me through the mechanics of how this workflow operates.

22:28.760 --> 22:35.420
Okay, so in this workflow, the designer begins with a corpus of previously developed human works.

22:36.080 --> 22:46.560
Let's say a population of 488 distinct academic papers, essays, or detailed data sets, all developed by a single author over their entire career.

22:47.120 --> 22:48.120
So it's their life's work.

22:48.400 --> 22:49.200
Basically, yes.

22:49.800 --> 22:54.480
The Python script is designed to treat this entire corpus as a population to be sampled.

22:54.480 --> 23:04.100
The code selects two or three items from those 488 works at random, and then it instructs the language model to propose a conceptual relation between them.

23:04.760 --> 23:06.140
So it isn't summarizing them.

23:06.720 --> 23:08.960
It's actively looking for synthesis.

23:09.360 --> 23:09.620
Yes.

23:10.420 --> 23:19.760
It might pull paper A from 2010 and paper B from 2024, and the prompt asks the AI, like, what is the unresolved tension between these two works?

23:19.760 --> 23:26.520
Or take the methodology used in paper A and apply it to the unresolved problem mentioned in the conclusion of paper B.

23:27.200 --> 23:28.420
Identify a boundary case.

23:28.620 --> 23:28.920
Exactly.

23:29.400 --> 23:37.880
It forces the model to perform the kind of associative, lateral thinking that a human scholar might experience during a sudden flash of insight in the shower.

23:38.100 --> 23:38.300
Right.

23:38.620 --> 23:42.980
The model exports detailed outlines and writing prompts based on these proposed relationships.

23:43.480 --> 23:48.480
Crucially, the script ensures that every single output carries strict provenance.

23:48.480 --> 23:54.980
It meticulously records exactly which of the 488 source texts were used to generate that specific outline.

23:55.420 --> 23:57.620
And this is where the math just explodes.

23:57.960 --> 24:03.500
Let's do the combinatorics here so we understand the sheer volume of this search space.

24:03.780 --> 24:04.280
Let's do it.

24:04.340 --> 24:15.200
If you have a pool of 488 eligible works and your script is looking for unordered pairs, just matching any two papers together, the formula is 488 choose 2.

24:15.620 --> 24:15.880
Right.

24:15.880 --> 24:20.280
That yields 118,828 possible pairs.

24:20.880 --> 24:28.940
And if the script is programmed to look for unordered triples, synthesizing three distinct papers, the math becomes 488 choose 3.

24:28.940 --> 24:34.720
Which results in 19,250,136 possible combinations.

24:35.160 --> 24:40.140
Almost 19 million potential source combinations, each generating a unique essay outline.

24:40.140 --> 24:41.340
It's mind-boggling.

24:41.740 --> 24:46.680
Now, the critical distinction FlyScan makes here is about intention and claims, right?

24:47.000 --> 24:52.520
Because a designer who builds and runs this workflow is not claiming to have authored 19 million essays.

24:52.640 --> 24:54.880
That would be an absurd, fraudulent claim.

24:55.140 --> 24:55.360
Right.

24:55.620 --> 25:00.380
No human lifespan could accommodate reading 19 million essays, let alone writing them.

25:00.640 --> 25:03.980
What the designer has authored is not a library of finished works.

25:04.180 --> 25:08.520
They have authored a procedure for entering a vast space of possible investigations.

25:08.520 --> 25:10.520
They authored this search space.

25:10.640 --> 25:10.920
Yes.

25:11.300 --> 25:14.340
They have defined the boundaries of an intellectual search space.

25:14.520 --> 25:19.740
That is such a vital concept to hold onto because it completely changes how we value the output.

25:19.740 --> 25:27.220
But, before we explore the economics of that search space, we have to look at the third workflow Flyxion details.

25:27.960 --> 25:29.120
Ah, Workflow 3.

25:29.660 --> 25:31.960
Because Workflow 3 is a satire.

25:32.320 --> 25:34.480
And it is a brilliant piece of performance art.

25:34.820 --> 25:38.680
But it reveals a deep vulnerability in our current academic infrastructure.

25:39.080 --> 25:42.080
It's hilarious, but also pretty terrifying.

25:42.800 --> 25:45.480
Workflow 3 is the cubic rhombus operation.

25:45.480 --> 25:57.140
This is a massive scale workflow dedicated entirely to generating preprint mathematical manuscripts about a completely invented fictional object called the cubic rhombus.

25:57.740 --> 26:00.100
A shape that literally does not exist in mathematics.

26:00.380 --> 26:00.560
Nope.

26:01.000 --> 26:06.220
And yet, this script churned out 14,000 preprints analyzing it.

26:06.320 --> 26:07.900
14,000.

26:08.240 --> 26:10.540
And they all share variations of the exact same title.

26:11.180 --> 26:14.260
A counterexample for the Euclidean cubic rhombus.

26:14.260 --> 26:22.340
And Flyxion points out that the immediate consequence of this workflow is the physical breaking of the infrastructure we rely on to host scholarship.

26:22.780 --> 26:23.660
It breaks the website.

26:23.940 --> 26:24.200
Yes.

26:24.820 --> 26:30.300
The public repository where these generated files are stored, the platform itself cannot handle the display.

26:30.960 --> 26:34.960
If you go to the directory listing, it truncates at exactly 1,000 files.

26:35.320 --> 26:36.780
The website literally gives up.

26:37.000 --> 26:41.940
You scroll to the bottom of the first 1,000 papers, then it just says 13,000 entries omitted.

26:41.940 --> 26:47.740
The digital architecture of academic sharing was fundamentally not built to withstand algorithmic volume.

26:48.160 --> 26:48.500
Not at all.

26:49.400 --> 26:54.140
But the true profundity of this satire lies in examining the code and the final output itself.

26:54.780 --> 27:05.300
Flyxion takes us deep into the repository, specifically analyzing an October 9, 2026 commit in the Flyxion math repository, looking at the final manuscript generated.

27:05.300 --> 27:11.680
And if you read the title of this manuscript, it sounds like the most advanced, intimidating mathematical breakthrough imaginable.

27:11.900 --> 27:12.920
Oh, it's packed with jargon.

27:13.120 --> 27:23.220
It references, quote, weighted geometry, toroidal topology, smooth regularity, periodic boundary conditions, and a computer-assisted technique.

27:23.220 --> 27:30.860
It is a masterpiece of dense academic jargon, but let's demystify what those terms mean in contrast to what the paper actually does.

27:30.980 --> 27:31.380
Please do.

27:31.780 --> 27:36.380
So, toroidal topology refers to the mathematical properties of a donut shape, a torus.

27:36.980 --> 27:40.640
Smooth regularity deals with surfaces that have no sharp points or cusps.

27:41.000 --> 27:42.640
It sounds incredibly complex.

27:42.640 --> 27:55.580
Yet, when you look at the table of parameters within the manuscript itself, the text explicitly admits that five of those seven incredibly complex-sounding concepts play absolutely no role in the argument.

27:56.000 --> 27:58.800
But the whole intimidating title is essentially a costume.

27:59.020 --> 27:59.600
Precisely.

27:59.960 --> 28:07.660
The entire manuscript boils down to a single elementary proposition about the determinant of a matrix with constant off-diagonal entries.

28:07.820 --> 28:08.520
Okay, wait, pause.

28:08.520 --> 28:18.320
For those of us who haven't taken linear algebra since college, what does the determinant of a matrix with constant off-diagonal entries actually mean in plain English?

28:18.840 --> 28:21.820
Why is it funny that they wrapped this up in toroidal topology?

28:22.320 --> 28:22.480
Right.

28:22.700 --> 28:22.920
Okay.

28:23.340 --> 28:26.400
A matrix is just a grid of numbers arranged in rows and columns.

28:26.900 --> 28:31.520
The diagonal is the line of numbers going from the top left to the bottom right.

28:31.640 --> 28:31.820
Okay.

28:32.380 --> 28:32.820
Got it.

28:33.300 --> 28:36.260
Off-diagonal entries are just all the other numbers in the grid.

28:36.260 --> 28:40.880
Constant means those other numbers are all the exact same value.

28:41.260 --> 28:41.580
Simple enough.

28:42.120 --> 28:52.100
And the determinant is simply a special single number you can calculate from that grid that tells you certain properties about it, like whether the equations it represents have a unique solution.

28:52.980 --> 28:59.540
Calculating the determinant of a matrix where almost all the numbers are the same is a known, relatively straightforward algebraic exercise.

28:59.900 --> 29:02.260
It is a tiny, simple math problem.

29:02.260 --> 29:10.200
So they took a basic algebra homework problem and they dressed it up in the language of advanced multidimensional topology.

29:10.580 --> 29:10.820
Yes.

29:11.040 --> 29:16.340
It's like putting a carbon fiber spoiler, racing stripes, and a jet engine decal on a push lawnmower.

29:16.480 --> 29:19.200
That is an excellent visualization of the absurdity.

29:20.080 --> 29:22.760
And the manuscript makes no attempt to hide this inflation either.

29:23.120 --> 29:29.580
It identifies itself internally as merely one of 256 manuscripts in its specific release class.

29:29.580 --> 29:36.100
But the absolute mic drop moment of this satire is the verification table appended to the very end of the paper.

29:36.560 --> 29:37.640
Oh, this is the best part.

29:38.000 --> 29:42.620
This is where the artifact violently discloses its own nature to the reader.

29:42.960 --> 29:43.840
It really does.

29:44.240 --> 29:46.980
The table lists human review as none.

29:47.520 --> 29:50.440
It lists independent scrutiny as none.

29:50.980 --> 29:53.720
It lists novelty as not assessed.

29:53.720 --> 29:58.720
And then, the manuscript closes with a blunt, undeniable statement.

29:59.360 --> 30:06.500
It explicitly states that the text does not support the hypothesis that it is an independent mathematical contribution.

30:07.020 --> 30:07.800
It just tells you.

30:07.800 --> 30:14.420
And it repeats this exact sentence in the abstract, the introduction, the discussion, and the final section.

30:14.800 --> 30:18.220
It is practically screaming at the reader, do not take me seriously.

30:18.480 --> 30:20.300
I am a product of sheer volume.

30:20.840 --> 30:24.200
And this is Fly Action's ultimate point with the cubic rhombus.

30:24.200 --> 30:29.740
This disclosure isn't a confession extracted from a guilty author who got caught cheating with AI.

30:30.520 --> 30:35.160
The disclosure is a built-in structural property of the artifact itself.

30:35.420 --> 30:42.700
The uniform structure, the repeated titles, the volume that breaks the hosting platform, together, they form a unified statement.

30:43.280 --> 30:47.440
The unit of interest here is not the individual 14,000th manuscript.

30:47.700 --> 30:48.980
The text is irrelevant.

30:49.620 --> 30:52.480
The unit of interest is the generator that produced it.

30:52.480 --> 31:00.600
It's performance art, proving that we now have the capability to generate infinitely more text than we can display, let alone read.

31:01.100 --> 31:04.320
Which kind of brings us to a massive economic question, doesn't it?

31:04.680 --> 31:05.140
It does.

31:05.680 --> 31:13.320
If the cubic rhombus proves we can break platforms with noise, what is the actual, tangible value of Workflow 2?

31:13.920 --> 31:18.380
What is the value of a machine churning out 19 million essay outlines?

31:19.300 --> 31:21.580
Is it just academic spam?

31:21.580 --> 31:24.220
That is the pivotal question of this era.

31:24.640 --> 31:28.660
And Bilaxian answers it by driving home a critical distinction that we must all adopt.

31:29.160 --> 31:32.400
A count of possible combinations is never a count of works.

31:32.620 --> 31:34.780
It is solely a count of opportunities.

31:35.260 --> 31:39.960
Because realistically, out of those 19 million outlines, how many are actually going to be good?

31:40.340 --> 31:44.180
The vast, overwhelming majority of them will be completely useless.

31:44.460 --> 31:44.580
Right.

31:44.580 --> 31:49.160
Many will be logically incoherent, hallucinating connections that just don't exist.

31:49.980 --> 31:56.380
Many will be entirely redundant, simply summarizing what the source text's already established without adding anything new.

31:57.260 --> 32:01.240
Many will be technically coherent, but just intellectually trivial.

32:01.240 --> 32:09.420
Like, the sky is blue in paper A, water is wet in paper B, conclusion generated, physical things have physical properties.

32:09.820 --> 32:10.160
Exactly.

32:10.740 --> 32:12.980
A perfect example of a trivial output.

32:13.520 --> 32:13.980
Useless.

32:14.820 --> 32:20.480
However, a very small, marginal fraction of those 19 million combinations might yield something brilliant.

32:20.700 --> 32:21.760
A diamond in the rough.

32:21.760 --> 32:22.320
Right.

32:22.940 --> 32:30.440
The AI might propose a juxtaposition, a tension between two obscure methodologies that neither source author anticipated.

32:30.960 --> 32:37.000
And that a human scholar might never have thought to combine on their own, due to the limits of human memory and association.

32:37.420 --> 32:37.520
Right.

32:37.600 --> 32:41.860
Because we just can't hold 488 papers in our active memory at once.

32:41.960 --> 32:42.260
Exactly.

32:42.760 --> 32:48.200
But identifying those few brilliant needles in that colossal haystack requires reading.

32:48.620 --> 32:49.500
And reading is slow.

32:49.500 --> 32:53.160
You know, hearing you say that, it makes me think of an analogy.

32:54.740 --> 33:05.000
Describing those 19 million generated outlines as 19 million essays, it's like walking into a massive, cavernous, newly built library.

33:05.160 --> 33:05.740
Okay, yeah.

33:06.040 --> 33:09.880
You look around at miles and miles of empty wooden shelves.

33:10.400 --> 33:14.660
And you turn to me and say, look, I have a library of 19 million books.

33:14.900 --> 33:15.800
But you don't.

33:16.260 --> 33:16.520
Right.

33:16.600 --> 33:17.620
You don't have books.

33:17.620 --> 33:19.500
You have shelf positions.

33:20.180 --> 33:22.680
You have the structural capacity to hold books.

33:23.180 --> 33:30.040
But until you actually fill those specific coordinates with written, vetted, readable text, you just have empty space.

33:30.040 --> 33:35.500
You are counting the geometry of the space, not the intellectual content within it.

33:35.500 --> 33:37.700
That is precisely the illusion of volume.

33:38.200 --> 33:48.560
And Flyxion actually elevates this concept by bringing in a very precise scientific parallel, which is combinatorial chemistry and the process of virtual screening in modern pharmacology.

33:48.560 --> 33:51.680
Oh, I don't know much about drug discovery.

33:51.680 --> 33:53.240
How does that parallel work?

33:53.240 --> 33:58.840
So in modern drug discovery, scientists don't just mix chemicals and beakers randomly anymore.

33:59.260 --> 34:04.340
They use software to generate an enormous, innumerable space of possible molecular compounds.

34:04.840 --> 34:09.340
They might map out millions and millions of potential chemical structures in silico.

34:09.700 --> 34:10.140
On the computer.

34:10.140 --> 34:16.900
Yes, but no chemist looks at that massive database and claims they have made a million scientific discoveries.

34:17.380 --> 34:22.120
They understand from the outset that this database is merely the boundary of a search space.

34:22.120 --> 34:28.040
It's a map of where they might look for a cure, not a catalog of cures they have already found.

34:28.040 --> 34:28.600
Exactly.

34:29.100 --> 34:40.480
They then use virtual screening, which are computational models that test how these millions of hypothetical molecules might bind to a specific disease target, like a lock and key enzyme model.

34:41.000 --> 34:41.080
Right.

34:41.660 --> 34:45.200
The software filters the millions down to a few hundred promising candidates.

34:45.200 --> 34:52.940
But even then, the actual discovery doesn't happen until a human synthesizes the compound in a physical lab and tests its efficacy.

34:53.820 --> 34:58.280
The massive generative search space merely optimized where human attention should be directed.

34:58.840 --> 34:59.040
Wow.

34:59.440 --> 35:09.400
So if generative models can instantly map the entire intellectual search space of 488 academic papers, the bottleneck of scholarship completely shifts.

35:09.780 --> 35:10.420
Fundamentally.

35:10.740 --> 35:14.460
Generative models change the fundamental economics of scholarly attention.

35:14.460 --> 35:15.440
How so?

35:16.020 --> 35:19.140
Well, for centuries, the primary bottleneck in academia was production.

35:19.640 --> 35:25.460
The physical time it took to research, type, rewrite, format, and publish an article constrained the volume of knowledge.

35:25.780 --> 35:27.840
But production's no longer the bottleneck.

35:28.280 --> 35:31.380
The rendering of prose is practically free and instantaneous now.

35:31.840 --> 35:32.100
Right.

35:32.320 --> 35:33.340
Hit a button and it's there.

35:33.740 --> 35:39.560
The binding constraint, the ultimate scarce resource in this new era, is the time of a competent human reader.

35:39.560 --> 35:46.700
Because only a human reader can look at an outline and definitively say, this is profound or this is trivial nonsense.

35:47.200 --> 35:50.840
The machine has provided a massive gain in the supply of candidate questions.

35:51.480 --> 35:53.280
It has not provided a supply of answers.

35:53.880 --> 36:01.540
Converting a candidate question into a genuine scholarly contribution depends entirely on human inspection, judgment, and endorsement.

36:01.940 --> 36:05.320
And human judgment remains strictly bounded by the number of hours in a day.

36:05.320 --> 36:10.080
This real estate brings us to a really uncomfortable dilemma regarding integrity, though.

36:10.520 --> 36:11.020
Oh, definitely.

36:11.520 --> 36:18.100
Because if we go back to Diaspermont, his whole solution for maintaining scholarly honesty was sentence-level vigilance.

36:18.460 --> 36:21.140
The author monitoring the AI word by word.

36:21.800 --> 36:30.060
But if the bottleneck is human attention and your output is 19 million outlines, you mathematically cannot maintain sentence-level vigilance.

36:30.300 --> 36:32.620
You die before you finish reading a fraction of it.

36:32.620 --> 36:32.980
Right.

36:33.920 --> 36:37.480
The old form of self-discipline is physically impossible at this scale.

36:37.620 --> 36:41.360
So how does a workflow designer actually maintain their integrity?

36:42.200 --> 36:45.520
Flyxion argues that the self-discipline of the scholar must evolve.

36:45.680 --> 36:46.600
It must move upstream.

36:47.400 --> 36:50.800
It can no longer be applied solely to the surface of the final prose.

36:51.160 --> 36:53.840
It must be applied to the pipeline itself.

36:54.140 --> 36:54.720
The pipeline.

36:55.220 --> 36:58.180
The discipline of the scholar becomes the discipline of the pipeline.

36:58.180 --> 37:06.740
And Flyxion lays out four rigorous new criteria for how a workflow designer must evaluate and report their own work to maintain intellectual honesty.

37:07.280 --> 37:07.300
Okay.

37:07.440 --> 37:15.520
Let's spend some time on these four criteria because this feels like the blueprint for how academic publishing is going to have to operate for the next century.

37:16.200 --> 37:18.100
Number one is specification.

37:18.640 --> 37:18.820
Right.

37:19.760 --> 37:25.320
Specification demands that the designer be acutely aware of what their curve dictates versus what it delegates.

37:25.660 --> 37:26.320
What do you mean by that?

37:26.320 --> 37:30.220
As a designer, what exact parameters did you fix in place?

37:30.460 --> 37:32.940
What temperature setting did you use for the language model?

37:33.460 --> 37:42.660
Did you force it to use specific vocabulary or did you deliberately leave the prompt open for the model to hallucinate or discover novel associations?

37:43.160 --> 37:43.240
Right.

37:43.660 --> 37:50.500
You must transparently document where your rigid instructions end and the model's probabilistic associations begin.

37:50.720 --> 37:51.520
That makes total sense.

37:51.820 --> 37:53.920
Number two is provenance.

37:53.920 --> 37:58.620
We touched on this with the 19 million machine, but why is it a core pillar of discipline?

37:59.060 --> 38:04.260
Because without strict provenance, the entire generative endeavor just collapses into unverifiable noise.

38:04.700 --> 38:10.060
Provenance means the sources must remain inextricably visible throughout every stage of the pipeline.

38:10.060 --> 38:12.700
So you always know where ideas came from.

38:13.100 --> 38:13.480
Exactly.

38:13.880 --> 38:20.900
When a reader or the designer themselves looks at a final output, they must be able to cleanly trace the lineage of the ideas.

38:21.340 --> 38:24.700
What concept came directly from the original human authored text?

38:25.020 --> 38:28.900
What transitional logic was supplied by the AI model's training data priors?

38:29.120 --> 38:29.380
Right.

38:29.380 --> 38:37.200
Without that map, you cannot verify the origin of the thought, and you risk plagiarizing the machine's hidden training data without even knowing it.

38:37.520 --> 38:37.660
Okay.

38:37.900 --> 38:40.080
Number three is the sampling regime.

38:40.340 --> 38:45.780
Since human attention is the bottleneck and you literally can't read 19 million outputs, you have to sample them.

38:46.120 --> 38:46.460
Correct.

38:47.020 --> 38:51.880
And the discipline here lies in the statistical honesty of how you inspect that sample.

38:52.140 --> 38:52.840
Give me an example.

38:52.840 --> 39:05.020
If you generate 19 million outlines, and you read exactly five of them, and three happen to be brilliant, you cannot turn around and claim that the entire generated body of 19 million works is brilliant.

39:05.280 --> 39:06.260
Because you don't know that.

39:06.620 --> 39:07.160
Exactly.

39:07.760 --> 39:14.540
Your claim of quality, utility, or novelty must be strictly bounded by the statistical reality of your inspection.

39:15.200 --> 39:17.900
You can only vouch for the percentage you have actually read.

39:18.360 --> 39:21.760
Which perfectly sets up the fourth and arguably most important criterion.

39:21.760 --> 39:23.900
Proportionate description.

39:24.620 --> 39:27.540
This feels like the ultimate honesty test for the modern scholar.

39:28.100 --> 39:28.740
It really is.

39:29.440 --> 39:34.440
Proportionate description requires the scholar to use language that accurately reflects the reality of their labor.

39:35.060 --> 39:38.940
It means presenting your workflow in four highly distinct categories.

39:39.460 --> 39:40.080
Walk us through them.

39:40.760 --> 39:43.360
First, here is the enumerated space.

39:43.640 --> 39:46.360
That's the mathematical bound of the 19 million possibilities.

39:46.900 --> 39:47.660
The empty shelves.

39:48.140 --> 39:48.420
Yes.

39:48.920 --> 39:51.500
Second, here are the generated outputs.

39:51.760 --> 39:55.740
That's the subset I actually spent compute credits to prompt the machine to write out.

39:56.540 --> 39:59.120
Third, here are the inspected outputs.

39:59.680 --> 40:03.540
This is the specific subset I actually sat down and read with my human eyes.

40:03.980 --> 40:07.400
And finally, fourth, here are the retained outputs.

40:07.400 --> 40:09.200
The ones that were actually good.

40:09.680 --> 40:10.040
Right.

40:10.220 --> 40:12.640
The handful that were actually profound enough to endorse.

40:13.440 --> 40:23.200
Blurring these categories, like implying you completed intellectual labor that was merely enumerated by code, that converts a brilliant piece of pipeline design into a fraudulent report.

40:23.200 --> 40:25.760
I want to push back on this framework a little bit, though.

40:26.180 --> 40:29.620
Let's say I'm an incredibly talented coder.

40:29.620 --> 40:34.080
I write a pipeline with a mathematically rigid specification.

40:34.540 --> 40:36.240
I lock down every single variable.

40:36.240 --> 40:44.000
I write a script so tight that it essentially forces the language model down a single, very narrow corridor of logic.

40:44.000 --> 40:48.080
Doesn't the tightness of my code solve the delegation problem?

40:48.520 --> 40:49.680
It seems like it would, right?

40:49.780 --> 40:49.980
Yeah.

40:50.200 --> 41:00.900
If I write a strict enough script, haven't I basically eliminated the AI's influence and therefore I can claim the output is purely 100% mine without needing all these sampling caveats?

41:00.900 --> 41:05.880
It is a logical assumption, but Flyxion's stance is a resounding no.

41:06.580 --> 41:10.720
Tightness of specification does not solve the fundamental issue of delegation.

41:11.340 --> 41:11.680
Why not?

41:11.920 --> 41:13.820
If I control the code, I control the thought.

41:14.080 --> 41:18.120
Because the separation of thought and rendering does not happen in the lines of code.

41:18.700 --> 41:26.880
Even a highly detailed, rigid specification can still hand monumental, intellectually consequential decisions over to a model.

41:27.360 --> 41:27.640
How?

41:27.640 --> 41:44.180
For example, if your strict code tells an AI to format a text perfectly into a 500-word summary, but allows the AI to decide which concepts to summarize to fit that word count, the AI is still making massive intellectual choices about what to omit.

41:44.780 --> 41:45.240
Oh, wow.

41:45.820 --> 41:47.720
I didn't think about omission as a choice.

41:48.760 --> 41:49.160
Exactly.

41:49.880 --> 41:56.660
Conversely, a very open, loose specification might be designed deliberately by the human to solicit unexpected discoveries.

41:56.660 --> 42:02.900
So the volume or strictness of the code doesn't correlate to the amount of humanness in the output.

42:03.500 --> 42:03.940
Precisely.

42:04.400 --> 42:08.260
You only own the thought if you review the rendering and endorse it as your own.

42:08.640 --> 42:11.480
The integrity doesn't come from writing an airtight script.

42:11.900 --> 42:19.880
It comes from the record of delegation, the transparency of what you asked the machine to do, and your subsequent endorsement of what you actually sat down and read.

42:19.880 --> 42:33.180
You know, if proportionate description, provenance, and honest sampling are the right ways to evaluate this new era of work, Flyxion argues we also have to turn a critical eye toward the wrong ways we currently evaluate work.

42:33.340 --> 42:36.900
And this part of the paper really shifted how I view academic arguments.

42:36.900 --> 42:45.820
It's a concept Flyxion calls the symmetry of the proxy, and it exposes this massive false purity test in our culture.

42:46.500 --> 42:51.020
It is perhaps the most incisive cultural critique in the entire essay, honestly.

42:51.300 --> 42:55.500
Let's revisit the stylistic proxy we discussed earlier, the M-dash.

42:55.500 --> 43:04.700
Right. We established that falsely accusing a text of being AI-generated just because it uses an M-dash or a tricolon is a bad proxy.

43:05.220 --> 43:10.200
It's a lazy, unscientific way to avoid actually engaging with the text's argument.

43:10.680 --> 43:20.100
Yes. But Flyxion argues that this bad proxy has a mirror image, a perfect reflection that functions in exactly the same way and is just as damaging to scholarly debate.

43:20.280 --> 43:20.800
What is it?

43:21.040 --> 43:23.900
And that is the declaration of human authenticity.

43:23.900 --> 43:25.700
You mean the badge of honor?

43:26.220 --> 43:30.180
The scholar proudly declaring, I wrote this with 100% no assistance.

43:30.480 --> 43:35.620
Free-range, organic, artisanally crafted human prose typed entirely by my own two hands.

43:35.820 --> 43:36.260
Exactly.

43:36.720 --> 43:42.280
When a scholar makes that declaration, Flyxion argues it functions identically to an accusation of AI use.

43:42.540 --> 43:49.600
I might need you to walk me through that because how is boasting about hard work the same as accusing someone of cheating?

43:50.120 --> 43:52.940
Because they are both just process claims.

43:52.940 --> 43:54.700
They are stories about origin.

43:54.880 --> 43:57.400
They are narratives about how the text came to exist.

43:58.160 --> 44:02.300
And as a reader, you cannot actually verify either of them just by looking at the artifact.

44:03.240 --> 44:09.660
More importantly, both narratives are deployed strategically to bypass the actual evaluation of the intellectual merit.

44:10.120 --> 44:10.660
Oh, I see.

44:10.900 --> 44:12.400
They are rhetorical shields.

44:12.680 --> 44:13.060
Exactly.

44:13.060 --> 44:15.440
Think about how these claims are weaponized out there.

44:15.440 --> 44:27.320
As Das Pramant pointed out, an accusation of AI use can be wielded by the establishment to easily dismiss disruptive, novel, or unpopular voices without ever having to debate their actual points.

44:27.320 --> 44:30.360
We don't need to engage with this radical new theory.

44:30.700 --> 44:32.460
It uses too many transition phrases.

44:32.960 --> 44:34.580
It was probably written by ChatGPT.

44:34.900 --> 44:35.140
Right.

44:35.240 --> 44:36.820
It is a silencing tactic.

44:37.100 --> 44:40.560
It protects the status quo by delegitimizing the challenger's tools.

44:41.220 --> 44:53.500
But on the flip side, the unexamined reputation for human authenticity is used as a shield to protect conventional, perhaps intellectually lazy, or deeply flawed scholarship from real critique.

44:53.500 --> 44:56.560
It creates an aura of unearned authority.

44:57.160 --> 44:59.960
Like, you can't criticize this paper's lack of original thought.

45:00.220 --> 45:03.700
It was painstakingly written by a tenured professor with a fountain pen.

45:04.180 --> 45:04.560
Exactly.

45:05.100 --> 45:07.220
It's the ultimate false purity test.

45:07.340 --> 45:10.000
We are judging the origins rather than the ideas.

45:10.580 --> 45:18.340
But the reality is, a complex, beautifully coded AI pipeline doesn't automatically confer brilliance onto a piece of logical garbage.

45:18.540 --> 45:18.760
No.

45:18.760 --> 45:24.520
But at the same time, writing with a quill by candlelight doesn't automatically make your ideas profound.

45:25.040 --> 45:28.240
The origin story does not dictate the value of the thought.

45:28.480 --> 45:31.260
You have hit the exact core of the argument.

45:31.940 --> 45:33.420
Process claims are secondary.

45:34.000 --> 45:43.620
Whether you are judging a single, hand-typed essay or a 19 million output generative workflow, the consistent, rigorous position must be the same.

45:43.620 --> 45:53.060
Examine the text, examine the design of the pipeline, examine the providence of the sources, and evaluate the actual, tangible thought presented.

45:53.660 --> 45:56.000
Origin is not destiny in scholarship.

45:56.440 --> 45:56.900
Well said.

45:57.400 --> 46:01.220
This has been an incredibly dense, genuinely paradigm-shifting journey today.

46:01.580 --> 46:04.420
Let's consolidate the core shift as we wrap up this deep dive.

46:04.620 --> 46:05.080
Good idea.

46:05.080 --> 46:07.780
So we started with the old rule, the baseline.

46:08.540 --> 46:16.200
For a single essay, the scholar must police the boundary between their thought and the machine's rendering, sentence by sentence, word by word.

46:16.920 --> 46:26.880
But as our unit of production inevitably shifts from the handcrafted essay to the massive generative workflow, our unit of scrutiny must shift alongside it.

46:26.880 --> 46:34.840
We can no longer rely on the rhythm of a sentence, the presence of an em-dash, or a boast of human purity to tell us if a text has value.

46:35.140 --> 46:37.180
We must expand our scrutiny upstream.

46:37.600 --> 46:44.080
We must look at the design of the pipeline, the strictness of the provenance, and the mathematical honesty of the sampling regime.

46:44.760 --> 46:48.000
But, and this is the crucial caveat Flyxion leaves us with,

46:48.720 --> 46:55.880
expanding our scrutiny to the workflow never, ever relieves anyone of the fundamental obligation to examine the individual work.

46:55.880 --> 46:56.720
Never.

46:57.040 --> 46:59.580
If you make a claim about an essay, you have to read the essay.

47:00.100 --> 47:03.200
If you make a claim about a generator, you have to examine its code.

47:03.960 --> 47:05.220
The obligations accumulate.

47:05.660 --> 47:06.820
They don't cancel each other out.

47:07.780 --> 47:09.360
Reading remains the bottleneck.

47:09.860 --> 47:14.000
Which brings us to a final, rather provocative thought to leave you with.

47:14.460 --> 47:21.820
A thought that builds on everything Flyxion has argued about human attention being the ultimate, unyielding, binding constraint of scholarship.

47:22.200 --> 47:23.380
I am bracing myself.

47:24.480 --> 47:25.660
What is the final thought?

47:25.880 --> 47:32.400
Well, Flyxion asserts that reading cannot be automated without importing the very question that needs answering.

47:33.100 --> 47:38.260
You cannot ask an AI to evaluate if an AI's essay is a brilliant human-level breakthrough,

47:38.580 --> 47:43.560
because you are relying on the machine's internal priors to judge the machine's own output.

47:43.780 --> 47:44.520
It's a closed loop.

47:45.060 --> 47:45.400
Exactly.

47:45.400 --> 47:53.360
But, if human attention is the absolute bottleneck in validating these millions of AI-generated hypotheses,

47:53.780 --> 47:58.220
how long until the sheer pressure of that volume forces our hand?

47:58.600 --> 48:00.360
Oh, I see where this is going.

48:00.360 --> 48:08.320
How long until we, as a society, overwhelmed by generated search spaces, are forced to build secondary AI workflows,

48:08.760 --> 48:13.560
designed not to generate the texts, but specifically to evaluate and endorse them on our behalf.

48:13.660 --> 48:14.220
Wow.

48:14.480 --> 48:16.740
To act as our proxies in the reading process.

48:16.740 --> 48:24.860
And if we'd reach that point, when the machine becomes both the author of the 19 million options and the sole reader capable of grading them,

48:25.440 --> 48:29.300
where exactly does the human discipline of thought reside?

48:29.300 --> 48:35.800
When the executive chef outsources both the cooking and the tasting, what is the chef actually doing?

48:37.020 --> 48:38.840
That is a haunting question to leave on.

48:39.400 --> 48:43.320
You know, we started this deep dive imagining you sitting at your desk, hitting run,

48:43.860 --> 48:47.580
and generating 19 million essays in an afternoon while you grabbed a coffee.

48:48.060 --> 48:48.340
Yeah.

48:48.820 --> 48:55.420
The terrifying and thrilling reality is that the tools to do exactly that are already here.

48:55.960 --> 48:57.360
The search space is open.

48:57.360 --> 49:04.120
The only question left is whether our human ability to judge the geometry of that space can catch up.

49:05.220 --> 49:06.880
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive.

